        "title": "McCloud's \"Understanding Comics\": Closure and Transitions",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|          SCOTT McCLOUD'S \"UNDERSTANDING COMICS\" (1993)           |\n|                                                                  |\n|  FORMAT: A comic book about comics (recursive self-reference)    |\n|  METHOD: Demonstrates claims through the medium it analyzes      |\n|                                                                  |\n|  CENTRAL CLAIMS:                                                 |\n|  1. Comics = \"juxtaposed pictorial and other images in           |\n|     deliberate sequence\" (definition)                            |\n|  2. Closure: reader fills the gap between panels                 |\n|  3. Panel transitions: 6 types; varying cognitive demand         |\n|  4. Blood in the gutter: violence as participatory experience    |\n|  5. Time and space: same thing in comics                         |\n|  6. Icon vs. reality: abstraction amplifies identification       |\n|                                                                  |\n|  SIGNIFICANCE: First systematic theory of the medium             |\n|  Limitation: Written before manga globalization; internet comics |\n|  Sequels: \"Reinventing Comics\" (2000); \"Making Comics\" (2006)    |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    }
